# Citation metadata for peaky. Powers GitHub's "Cite this repository" widget
# and seeds the Zenodo record created from a GitHub Release.

cff-version: 1.2.0
title: peaky
message: >-
  If you use peaky in your research, please cite it using the metadata in this
  file.
type: software
abstract: >-
  AI-native analysis toolbox for Mascope. Describe what you want in plain language
  and get reproducible peak assignments, figures, and reports from high-resolution
  CIMS mass-spec data without writing notebook code.
authors:
  - given-names: Aleksei
    family-names: Shcherbinin
    email: aleksei.shcherbinin@ultratrace.eu
    affiliation: Ultra Trace Systems Oy
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0002-5128-5627"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ultra-trace-systems/peaky"
url: "https://github.com/ultra-trace-systems/peaky"
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.6.0
date-released: "2026-08-12"
# `version` + `date-released` name the last release that was actually TAGGED,
# published and archived - what a citation has to resolve to. They therefore LAG
# `pyproject.toml`, which carries the version under development, and that gap is
# correct, not drift: as of 0.7.0 in pyproject, the newest cut release is 0.6.0.
# Bump these two in the release-prep commit, to the version and date of the
# `## [x.y.z] - <date>` CHANGELOG heading for the release being cut -- which
# tests/test_versioning.py checks, so a wrong or invented date fails a test.
# `doi` is the concept DOI - it always resolves to the latest archived release
# and is what the README badge links to. The per-version DOI exists only after
# Zenodo archives the published release, so it is not tracked here; pick it off
# the Zenodo record's version list when citing one exact release.
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21058928"
